```sql
--Description / thinking process:
-- We need to see, for every item a picker had to retrieve, how much the customer had actually ordered.
-- The table picking_line tells us which product (product_id) and which customer order (order_id) the picker is working on.
-- The orderlines table holds the original ordered quantity (qty) for that same order_id + product_id pair.
-- Therefore, a straightforward join on order_id and product_id gives us the “required” (ordered) quantity
-- for each individual pick-line.  We choose columns that truly exist (picklist_id & line_no),
-- thereby avoiding the earlier “no such column” error that arose from referencing pl.id.
SELECT  pl."picklist_id",
        pl."line_no",
        pl."order_id",
        pl."product_id",
        ol."qty" AS "required_qty"
FROM    "picking_line" AS pl
JOIN    "orderlines"   AS ol
      ON ol."order_id"   = pl."order_id"
     AND ol."product_id" = pl."product_id"
LIMIT 20;
```
